WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.410
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:10.070
There are many AI techniques that are able
to look at a still image, and identify objects,

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.809
textures, human poses and object parts in
them really well.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:19.769
However, in the age of the internet, we have
videos everywhere, so an important question

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:23.390
would be how we could do the same for these
animations.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.949
One of the key ideas in this paper is that
the frames of these videos are not completely

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:33.469
independent, and they share a lot of information,
so after we make our initial predictions on

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:39.500
what is where exactly, these predictions from
the previous frame can almost always be reused

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.449
with a little modification.

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:46.199
Not only that, but here you can see with these
results that it can also deal with momentary

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:50.649
occlusions and is ready to track objects that
rotate over time.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:56.390
A key part of this method is that one, it
looks back and forth in these videos to update

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:03.270
these labels, and second, it learns in a self-supervised
manner, which means that all it is given is

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:08.410
just a little more than data, and was never
given a nice dataset with explicit labels

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.020
of these regions and object parts that it
could learn from.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.300
You can see in this comparison table that
this is not the only method that works for

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:22.390
videos, the paper contains ample comparisons
against other methods and comes out ahead

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:28.060
of all other unsupervised methods, and on
this task, it can even get quite close to

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.850
supervised methods.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.890
The supervised methods are the ones that have
access to these cushy labeled datasets and

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.470
therefore should come out way ahead.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.000
But they don’t, which sounds like witchcraft,
considering that this technique is learning

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.360
on its own.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.380
However, all this greatness comes with limitations.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.730
One of the bigger ones is that even though
it does extremely well, it also plateaus,

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:57.420
meaning that we don’t see a great deal of
improvement if we add more training data.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.500
Now whether this is because it is doing nearly
as well as it is humanly, or computerly possible,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:08.550
or because a more general problem formulation
is still possible remains a question.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.700
I hope we find out soon.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.349
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

